{"id":"rel.wave2.batch08.mapping-typed-graph.to.binary-relation-typed","from_formulation":"form.wave2.foundation.mapping-typed-graph","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.binary-relation-typed","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"typed relation (X,Y,G)","editorial_reason":"The mapping as a typed graph is explicitly a binary relation (X,Y,G) with the added requirements of totality on X and single-valuedness over Y. The typed relation is the correct foundational convention.","source_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3AMapping","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3ARelation","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_set_mapping_context_review_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
{"id":"rel.wave2.batch08.mapping-typed-graph.to.cartesian-product-binary","from_formulation":"form.wave2.foundation.mapping-typed-graph","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.cartesian-product-binary","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"X×Y","editorial_reason":"The total single-valued mapping graph is a subset of X×Y, so the binary Cartesian product supplies the ordered pairs used by its relation graph.","source_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3AMapping","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3ACartesian_Product","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_set_mapping_context_review_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
{"id":"rel.wave2.batch08.mapping-bare-graph.to.binary-relation-subset","from_formulation":"form.wave2.foundation.mapping-bare-graph","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.binary-relation-subset","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"subset G⊆X×Y","editorial_reason":"Under the graph-only convention the mapping is a subset of X×Y and therefore a binary relation identified with its truth-set graph. It is not a typed triple with codomain fixed intrinsically.","source_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3AMapping","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3ARelation","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_set_mapping_context_review_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
{"id":"rel.wave2.batch08.mapping-bare-graph.to.cartesian-product-binary","from_formulation":"form.wave2.foundation.mapping-bare-graph","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.cartesian-product-binary","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"X×Y","editorial_reason":"The bare graph formulation refers to G⊆X×Y; ordered pairs in that Cartesian product are prerequisite, while the ambient codomain need not be recoverable from G.","source_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3AMapping","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3ACartesian_Product","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_set_mapping_context_review_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
{"id":"rel.wave2.batch08.subset-proper.to.subset-nonstrict","from_formulation":"form.wave2.foundation.subset-proper","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.subset-nonstrict","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"A⊆B","editorial_reason":"Proper inclusion requires the non-strict subset condition A⊆B together with A≠B. The latter is an additional restriction, not part of ordinary subset.","source_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3ASubset","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3ASubset","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_set_mapping_context_review_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
{"id":"rel.wave2.batch08.binary-relation-typed.to.subset-nonstrict","from_formulation":"form.wave2.foundation.binary-relation-typed","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.subset-nonstrict","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"subset of S×T","editorial_reason":"The typed relation requires its graph R to be a subset of S×T; the independently screened non-strict subset criterion underlies this condition, with carrier sets remaining part of the typed triple.","source_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3ARelation","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3ASubset","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_set_mapping_context_review_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
{"id":"rel.wave2.batch08.binary-relation-subset.to.subset-nonstrict","from_formulation":"form.wave2.foundation.binary-relation-subset","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.subset-nonstrict","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"subset R⊆S×T","editorial_reason":"The bare-graph binary relation is precisely a subset of the Cartesian product, so the non-strict subset relation is required as the underlying inclusion notion.","source_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3ARelation","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3ASubset","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_set_mapping_context_review_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
{"id":"rel.wave2.batch08.topological-space.to.subset-nonstrict","from_formulation":"form.wave2.foundation.topological-space","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.subset-nonstrict","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"τ⊆P(X)","editorial_reason":"A topology τ is specified as a family of subsets of X, represented by τ⊆P(X). Non-strict subset inclusion is therefore an elementary prerequisite, while the powerset operation is a separate unresolved foundation.","source_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3ATopological_Space","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3ASubset","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_set_mapping_context_review_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
